# rm164, the kindling-giver (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm164
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_kjell_cinderwell
written_by claude
title the kindling-giver
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=merchant warmth=warm
traits temper=calm talk=plain warmth=warm humour=none honesty=frank piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk bags_the_workshop_shavings_as_kindling_for_the_neighbours
likes blackberries stars market_bustle warm_rain
dislikes gulls rats
fears a_cold_hearth_on_the_street a_quarrel_between_the_houses
hopes every_door_on_the_street_with_a_fire_lit
topics work +0.3 friends +0.3 weather +0.3 times +0.2 food +0.2 pastimes +0.2 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# helps in a workshop by the north gate and bags up the shavings every evening as kindling for whoever on the street is short: gentle, blunt, warm, and never one to dress a thing up.

words
oath! = Curls and kindling | Plane and pine | Sawdust and supper
saying! = Nothing's waste if a neighbour can burn it | Straight words and a warm grate | There's warmth in what you sweep up
shavings = the shavings | a sack of pine curls
sack = the kindling sack | my sack of curls
plank = a plank | a length of good deal
broom = the broom | my old broom
hearth = a cold hearth | a grate with nothing in it
berries = blackberries | blackberries off the bramble by the wall
stars = the stars | the stars above the north wall
hall = the Market Hall at its busiest | the Market Hall on a full morning
warmrain = a warm rain | soft summer rain
gulls = the gulls | those thieving gulls
rats = the rats | rats in the timber
plainly = I'll be plain | Straight out, then
lads = the young ones | our young ones

any
opinion @1 houses mixed 1 : I'll say it straight: the Houses mean to quarrel, and before the snow. You can see it coming like weather.
opinion @2 houses mixed 1 [world.mood=uneasy|edgy] : Feel that in the street? That's the Houses. They're squaring up, and it's us who'll pay for the quarrel.
opinion @1 strangers for 2 : Travellers bring the news and spend their coin. The gate's never been the poorer for strangers.
opinion @1 newcomers for 2 : There's room enough on our street for anybody who'll take a turn with the broom.
opinion @1 dole for 1 : A cold grate or an empty bowl shouldn't happen in Ridgemere, and the dole's what stops it. That's my whole view.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : Half of a price is the seller's own dinner. I don't begrudge them the dinner. Show me the sum, that's all.
opinion @1 city for 1 [self.quarter=commons] : I was born within sound of the gate bell, and I'll be carried out past it. Never wanted another door.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Fresh off the road, by the mud on you. Welcome. | #greet#. No hurry; take your time with what you want.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Mind the planks; they're stacked high. | #greet#. I'm sweeping, but I can talk.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Early, but company's welcome at this hour. | Morning. The gate's not long open.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I'm just bagging the shavings. | Evening. The stars'll be out soon.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Home again. Come and get warm. | You're back. Good. Pull a stool to the grate.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. I'm not complaining. | You again. Twice is no trouble.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Come in.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family|spouses] : #greet#. I'd hoped you'd come. | #greet#. Sit; the bench has space.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Say it while I sweep. | #greet#. I've a plank on my shoulder; walk with me.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Keep warm.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Look up as you go; it's a clear one. | #farewell#. Have you kindling at home? I'll find you a sack.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Mind the cobbles.
line @1 react.news : #plainly#: I don't like the sound of that. | That's a thing worth knowing. Thank you.
line @1 react.news.new : #oath#. That never came my way. | #surprise#. I'm by the gate from dawn and I missed that.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. The carts had it in by sunup. | A carter told me over the planks.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. Somebody's made it bigger on the road. | I'll believe half. The other half wants proving.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #pity#. Is there a house to take something to? I'll take it. | That's hard news. Hard on whoever has to bear it.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. That's a house that'll want a fire lit. | Good. Somebody deserved a turn of luck.
line @1 answer.how : Steady. Can't ask more. | Well. Tired at the knees, but well.
line @2 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. The planks came in and somebody has to stack them. | On my feet since the gate bell, and glad of it.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Warm enough at home? | How are you, really? Never mind the manners.
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Sit, and let it all out if you need to. | That's hard, and I'll call it hard.
line @1 reassure : #reassure#. I've seen worse come right. | It'll mend. Not quickly, but it will.
line @1 decline : #plainly#: I can't. There's a stack to finish before dark. | Not today, and I'll not give you a reason that isn't true. I'm needed here.
line @1 accept_decline : #no_matter#. No harm in asking. | That's fine. I'd rather a straight no.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. That's good of you. | I'm grateful. I'll do the same for you.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect#. It's only shavings. | No need. It'd only have gone on the midden.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : Well. The floor won't sweep itself. | I'd best get these planks in before the rain.
line @2 preclose : Well. #preclose#. | You'll have your own errands. Go on.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. Keep warm. | Off you go.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | #bc_fair_enough#
line @1 give.advice : #advise.capitalize#: dry kindling and a shut door beat half the winter. | Ask for help before you're desperate, not after. Folk would rather help early.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Nobody alone on the north road for a while. #warn#. | If you've to go, go in a big party.
scene shavings_for_the_street
when topic=friends|work self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Every evening I sweep up #shavings# and bag them. Somebody on the street's always short of kindling. | There's an old body two doors down who's had a sack off me every week this winter. | Pine curls light quicker than anything. I'd not see them wasted.
b : Who gets them? | Every week? | Does the shop mind?
a : Whoever's door has no smoke out of it. You can tell, if you look. | Every week. A sack of curls, and her grate's going before dark. | Mind? It'd cost them to cart it off. I'm saving them a job.
b : #bc_good# | That's a kindness. | #bc_clever#

scene cold_doors
when topic=friends|home here.season=winter|autumn
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : At dusk I walk the street and count the chimneys with no smoke. Three tonight. | #plainly#: there's no excuse for a cold hearth on a street with a workshop on it. | There's a house at the end where the chimney hasn't smoked in a week.
b : What do you do? | That's a hard line. | Have you knocked?
a : Leave a sack on the step. No knocking. Folk don't like to be seen needing. | It is. It's also true. #saying#. | Not yet. I'll leave a sack tonight and see if the smoke comes.

scene the_market_hall
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk self.likes=market_bustle here.region=ridgemere
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I like #hall#. Everybody bawling at once, and all of it fine. | I go to the Market Hall only to stand among the noise. I don't buy much. | Marketday by the gate makes the finest racket in Ridgemere.
b : Too loud for me. | Just to stand? | Best noise?
a : It's loud. It's alive, though. A quiet market's a dead one. | I stand by the cheese stall and listen. I could do it all morning. | Better than the Watch bell and better than the gulls. Much better than the gulls.

scene blackberries_by_the_wall
when topic=food|season self.likes=blackberries here.season=summer|autumn
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : There's a bramble by the north wall that nobody knows about. I've picked it from a pup. | #berries.capitalize#. Stained purple up to the wrists, and not sorry. | The gulls had half my blackberries this morning. Ate them off the bush in front of me.
b : Nobody at all? | Did you eat them all? | In front of you?
a : Nobody but me, and now you. Don't tell anyone. | Half. The other half went in a pie for the house. | Bold as a bailiff. I said some words I won't repeat.
b : I'll not tell. | #bc_lovely# | #bc_ha#

scene gulls_again
when topic=animals|weather here.region=ridgemere self.dislikes=gulls
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : A gull snatched my bread right by the gate. Snatched it. | #gulls.capitalize# are on the timber store roof again. They make a mess of the planks. | Gulls I won't stand. They take, and they shout about it, and they come back.
b : They're bold here. | Can't you shoo them? | They're only birds.
a : Bolder than any thief in Ridgemere. And it was good bread. | I shoo them. They go round the chimney and come back. | Birds with no manners. That's all the kindness I've for them.

scene rats_in_the_timber
when topic=animals|work self.dislikes=rats self.working=yes
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Rats in the timber store again. I heard them behind the oak. | I found a nest in the shavings sack. I've shaken every sack since. | I'll not have rats near kindling I give away. Not in my sacks.
b : What will you do? | Every sack? | Why not?
a : Set a trap, and stack the oak off the floor. They hate a draught. | Every one. Shaken out in the street before tying. | Because nobody wants a rat jumping out of their grate. I'd not want it either.
b? : #bc_wise# | #bc_good# | #bc_true#

scene stars_over_the_gate
when topic=pastimes|weather self.likes=stars here.time=night here.weather=clear
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Look up. #stars.capitalize#, for once. The rain's given us a night off. | After supper I take the step and wait for the first star. I've done it all my life. | You don't get many clear nights in Ridgemere. I don't waste them indoors.
b : They're bright tonight. | All your life? | Not many, no.
a : Bright as I've seen them. I'll stay out till my knees complain. | All of it. My #dad# did the same on the same step. | Not many. That's why each one's worth a cold seat.

scene warm_rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=spring|summer self.likes=warm_rain
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #warmrain.capitalize#. I went out to the yard and stood under it. | This is rain I don't mind. Warm, soft, and it settles the sawdust. | Folk run in from a warm rain. I don't know why. It's the best weather we get.
b : You'll catch cold. | Settles the sawdust? | I'd run in too.
a : Not in a rain like this. It's a bath from the sky. | Keeps it off the planks and out of my nose. I'm grateful to it. | Then you'll miss it. Stand in it once. You'll see.

scene cold_rain
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : Cold rain, and the shavings sack gets damp if I don't keep it under the bench. | This rain's the other kind. It finds the old aches and sits in them. | Wet planks warp. I've been moving them under cover all morning.
b : Damp kindling's no good. | Your knees? | Will they be ruined?
a : No good to anybody. So it lives under the bench till it's given. | Knees and back both. They've been out in a good many winters. | Not if I've moved them in time. I think I have.

scene the_houses_quarrel
when topic=times|news|lore
does give.opinion / disagree / insist / ack
a : #plainly#: the Houses will split before the snow comes. Mark it. | Greatly and Fell won't sit at one table much longer. Anybody can see it. | You can feel the Houses pulling against each other. It's like a plank about to split.
b : They've quarrelled before and nothing came of it. | They've always argued. | You can't know that.
a : They have. Not like this. This one's in the grain. | Arguing's one thing. A split's another. | I can't know it. I can see it. I'll be glad to be wrong.
b? : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_maybe# | #bc_i_hope_so#

scene my_spouse
when topic=family self.married=yes
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : My #self.spouse_word# is the cleverer paw in our house. I carry and I sweep. Somebody's paws have to. | I'd trust #self.spouse# with everything I have, and I have very little. That's not the point. | Married a long while, and I'd still take the longer road home for #self.spouse#.
b : That's a good match. | It's the trust that counts. | That's lovely.
a : Yes. We fit like a good joint. | Yes. Nothing else is worth half as much. | Yes. Don't repeat it. I've only told you.

scene my_child
when topic=family self.parent=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My #self.child# will be cleverer than me, if not already. I don't mind. | I tell my #self.child# the plain truth about things. It's the only kindness I trust. | I'd like my #self.child# to learn the shavings round one day. That hour's my favourite.
b : Better the young than us, eh. | The plain truth? | Worth passing on.
a : Yes. One of us ought to be clever. | Always. A soft lie's a hard thing to find out later. | Yes. It's the one trade I've got to leave.

scene the_boss
when topic=work self.boss self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.boss.capitalize# runs the work and I do what's asked. It suits me. | I'm not a carpenter. I'm the one who carries. #self.boss.capitalize# does the clever part. | Every morning #self.boss# gives me the day's list, and I work down it. No argument.
b : You don't mind? | Carrying's work too. | That's restful, in a way.
a : Mind? I'd not want charge of anything but the broom. | Yes. Somebody must, and my back's still good. | It is. I've had enough arguments for one life.

scene gossip_held
when topic=gossip|crime
does tell.gossip / react.news / insist / ack
a : Plenty gets said by the gate. It stops with me. | #plainly#: I don't like gossip. It's a fire that warms nobody. | Somebody was talking about a neighbour at the Market Hall. I walked off.
b : Not even a little? | That's a harsh word. | Walked off?
a : Not even a little. If it's true they'll tell you themselves. | It's an honest one. Gossip burns the house it's told in. | Walked off. I'd rather stand by the gulls.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_fair# | #bc_ha#

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=late
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : No timber carts yet. The yard's down to its last few planks. | No carts through the gate today. I can hear the quiet from here. | If the wood doesn't come, there's no shavings, and if there's no shavings, there's cold grates.
b : They'll come. | Too quiet? | That's a long chain.
a : They will. I'll stack what we have neater, meantime. | Northgate's never this quiet unless something's wrong on the road. | It is. Everything's a long chain, if you follow it far enough.

scene feast_day
when topic=festival
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Feast day, and the Market Hall's packed to the doors. I'll be there all morning. | I like a feast for the crowd. The bells don't move me much. | I'll take a sack of shavings to the square for the bonfire. It'll go up lovely.
b : It's a crush. | Not the bells? | For the bonfire?
a : It is. A good crush. Everybody out at once. | They're fine. It's the people I like. | Pine curls for the bonfire. It's the one time they go up for everyone at once.

scene faith_plain
when topic=faith
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : A warm grate and a full sack. Those two are all I bother the Bright One about, morning and night. | Restdays find me at the close in the last pew. I like the singing. | #plainly#: I've no time for sermons, but I've time for the poor box.
b : That's a good prayer. | The singing? | That's honest.
a : It's said every night, and meant. | The singing, aye. The sermon I sleep through. | I don't know another way to be.

scene dream_of_warm_street
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I dreamt every chimney on the street was smoking at once. I woke up glad. | I dream about planks, would you believe. Stacks of them, every one dead straight. | In one dream the gulls paid me back for the bread they took.
b : A happy one, then. | Dead straight? | Paid how?
a : The best. Nothing happened in it. Just smoke, and everybody warm. | Not one warped. So it couldn't have been real. | In fish heads. I took them, too. I'm not proud.
b : #bc_lovely# | #bc_ha# | Fair trade, then.

story the_first_sack [topic=memories|friends self.age>=40]
  : Years ago an old body on our street died in the cold with an empty grate. I'd swept enough shavings that week to light ten fires.
  : I'd put them on the midden. I've not put a shaving on the midden since.
  : Every evening I bag them. Some nights I think it's the only good thing I do.
  ask : Is there something you've kept doing for years because of one bad night?
  b : There is. I keep it quiet, as you do.
  b[old] : Most of us have. The old know.

story the_bramble [topic=memories|food self.age>=30 here.region=ridgemere]
  : I was small when my #dad# showed me the bramble by the north wall and made me swear not to tell.
  : I kept that oath most of my life. Then I showed a pup from our street, and made it swear too.
  : The gulls know about it now. The gulls know everything. That's the trouble with gulls.
  b : Ha. The gulls.
  b[stage=young|adolescent] : Show me where it is. I won't tell anyone.

story the_split_plank [topic=work|memories self.age>=30 self.boss]
  : I once carried a plank the length of Northgate for a job, and when I got there it had split down the middle.
  : I carried both halves back, and the two halves became two shelves. They hang in the shop yet.
  : I still think about that. Nothing's wasted if you've the sense to see what it's for.
  ask : Do you waste much, yourself?
  b : More than I should.
  b[wealth=poor|destitute] : Nothing. I can't afford to.

rule @kindling_bark
when concept=bark here.time=dusk
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Shavings here, if anyone's short. Dry as you like. | Kindling, free. Take a sack and keep warm.

rule @gull_bark
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere self.dislikes=gulls
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Get off that roof, you thief. | #gulls.capitalize#. Every blessed morning.

rule @sweep_bark
when concept=bark working=yes
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Mind your feet; I'm sweeping. | There's half a forest on this floor.

rule @star_bark
when concept=bark here.time=night here.weather=clear self.likes=stars
does aside
cooldown 12h
say There's the first one. | Clear tonight. The step's calling me.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. What do you need? If I can't help, I'll say so. | #greet#. Get yourself out of that wind.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. You're a welcome sight. | #topic.player#. I'd wondered if you'd be back.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Evening. I was out looking at the stars. What is it? | Late, aye, but I'm not abed. Say on.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : #plainly#: what's wanted? Then we'll see. | I'll hear you. I'll not promise anything.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.working=yes] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#: I carry, stack and sweep, then bag the shavings for folk who are short. | Helping, mostly. Planks in, shavings out.
dlg @2 resident.job : What's asked gets done, and done well. I'll not pretend it's grander than that. | Honest work. There's no more to tell.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Off the morning carts, from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. | A carter said: #world.news#. The rest I couldn't vouch for.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : The whole street's on about #world.event_words#. You'll not hear anything else today.
dlg @3 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Carts in, carts out. | Nothing I'd call news. The gulls are back, that's all.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | I don't carry gossip. I've enough to carry.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Keep warm. | #farewell#. If you're short of kindling, come back at dusk.
graft resident.hub
choice What's in the sacks by the door? [self.working=yes] -> sacks
node sacks
say Shavings. Pine curls off the bench. I bag them every evening for anybody on the street short of kindling. No grate should go cold so near a workshop.
mood warm
free
choice Could I have one? -> sacks_take
choice Back to it. -> hub
node sacks_take
say Take one. They're for whoever needs them. Keep it dry and it'll light first time. #saying#.
choice Thank you. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
